    {
      "id": "p1",
      "text": "In the crafting of a border enforcement strategy, the data showed an upward shift in apprehensions along the southwest border during spring. The seasonal climate acted as a catalyst for movement, prompting a surge in the flow of people. Yet lawmakers remained deadlocked, creating a state of legislative stalemate over funding for the very agencies tasked with managing this numerical change. The system's intended effect achieved was a sustained state of reduced crossing, a consequence of severe restriction that defined the year's immigration statistics.",
